#!
.fullspan
  .center
    ![Logo](../image/new_logo.png)

    ---
    #! Groundwater -- Principles
    ###! EMSC3025/6025
    ---

    ###! _Dr. Sia Ghelichkhan_

# Objectives

.row
  .container width:{100%}
    By the end of this module, you will be able to:

    - **Porosity and conductivity**: explain why porosity tells you how much water a rock holds, but not how fast the rock releases it
    - **Hydraulic head**: explain why head controls the direction of flow and pressure does not, and calculate head from a field measurement
    - **Darcy's law**: calculate specific discharge and pore velocity, and state when the law fails
    - **Origin of Darcy's law**: show why conductivity increases with the square of the grain size, and use this to explain the empirical formulas for $ K $
    - **Layered ground**: calculate the equivalent horizontal and vertical conductivity, and explain how layering alone can make horizontal conductivity a million times larger than vertical conductivity
    - **Field data**: interpret piezometer nests, potentiometric maps and water-level hydrographs
    - **GRACE**: explain how satellite gravity measures total water storage, and how the groundwater part is separated from it


# Two separate questions

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    This lecture answers two questions about a volume of soil or rock.

    .box title:{1. How much water does it hold?}
      **Porosity** answers this. It depends on the amount of void space.

    .box title:{2. How fast can the water flow out?} background:{.lightblue}
      **Hydraulic conductivity** answers this. It depends on how the voids connect.

    A common mistake is to assume that the answer to one question gives the
    answer to the other. It does not. Clay has one of the highest porosities
    of the materials in this lecture, and one of the lowest conductivities.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Wetland](../image/internet_wetland_info.png "From wetlands to aquifers: this module is about what happens between the two.")


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Porosity: how much water the ground holds


# Porosity of a Soil or Rock

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Porosity** is the fraction of a volume of rock or soil that is void space.
    - **Total porosity** $ n_T $ is defined as:

      $ n_T = \frac{V_v}{V_T} = \frac{V_T - V_s}{V_T} $

      where $ V_v $ is the volume of voids, $ V_s $ the volume of solids and $ V_T $ the total volume.
    - Since $ V_v < V_T $, porosity is always a fraction below 1. It is often quoted as a percentage.
    - In the laboratory it is easier to weigh than to measure void volume, so the same quantity is obtained from densities:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\rho_b}{\rho_s} $

      with $ \rho_b $ the **bulk density** of the dry sample and $ \rho_s $ the **particle density** of the solids.
    - For most silicate minerals $ \rho_s \approx 2650 $ kg/m³, so a dry bulk density of 1600 kg/m³ gives $ n_T = 0.40 $.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Porosity Schematic](../image/internet_porosity.jpg "Void space is what holds the water. How the voids connect is a separate question.")


# What sets porosity: packing

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - For grains of **one size**, porosity is set by how they are stacked.
    - Take identical spheres. The loosest stable stacking is cubic, each sphere sitting directly on the one below:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\pi}{6} = 0.476 $

    - The tightest is rhombohedral, each sphere resting in the hollow between three others:

      $ n_T = 1 - \frac{\pi}{3\sqrt{2}} = 0.260 $

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both numbers are **independent of the size of the spheres**. Void volume and total volume both increase with the cube of the radius, so their ratio stays the same. A box of marbles and a box of beach balls have the same porosity.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Sphere packing and porosity](../image/franklin_fig_3-2.png "Sections of four contiguous spheres of equal size: in (a) the most compact arrangement and the lowest porosity; in (b) less compact; in panel c, least compact and the highest porosity.")

    Compaction pushes the grains of a real sediment from the loose arrangement towards the tight one. This lowers the porosity without changing the grain size.


# What sets porosity: sorting and shape

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - **Sorting** is the spread of grain sizes in a sample.
      - **Well sorted** (one size): porosity is close to the sphere-packing values on the previous slide.
      - **Poorly sorted** (mixed sizes): small grains fill the voids between large ones, and porosity is lower.
      - Glacial till, a mix of sand, silt and clay, is the usual example. Its mean porosity is 23.5%, the lowest of the unconsolidated materials in the porosity table later in this section.
    - **Grain shape** can raise or lower porosity. Angular and flat grains can prop each other up and keep voids open that spheres would fill. They can also fit together tightly and close voids that spheres would leave open. Shape alone does not tell you which effect wins.
    - **Clays** reach 40 to 55%. Surface charges hold their flat particles edge to face, which gives an open structure with a lot of void space. The small size of the particles is not the reason.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{A common misconception} background:{.lightred}
      In the table of measured values, finer materials tend to have higher porosity. This is a pattern in the data. It does not mean that small grains cause high porosity. In a well-sorted material, grain size has no effect on porosity. Clay is porous because of the shape and charge of its particles.

    Grain size does control **conductivity**: later in this lecture we show that $ K $ increases with the square of the grain diameter.


# Primary and Secondary Porosity

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - **Primary porosity**: the voids that formed with the rock.
      - Pores between grains in a sediment or sandstone
      - Vesicles, lava tubes and cooling fractures in basalt
    - **Secondary porosity**: voids created afterwards.
      - Joints and faults in igneous, metamorphic and consolidated sedimentary rock
      - Solution-enlarged openings in carbonates and other soluble rock
    - The two types behave differently. Primary porosity is spread through the whole rock. Secondary porosity is concentrated in fractures and openings, which may or may not connect to each other.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(100%)[Types of openings](../image/franklin_fig_3-1.png "Types of openings in water-bearing rocks. (a) is a few millimetres wide and shows primary porosity in unlithified sand. panels b and c are metres to tens of metres wide: fracturing in granite, and caverns in limestone produced by solution along fractures.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{2px}
      The name of a rock, for example "limestone", tells you little about its porosity. Its history of fracturing and dissolution tells you much more.


# Porosity in natural materials

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    | Material          | Min    | Mean  | Max  | SD   |
    |-------------------|--------|-------|------|------|
    | **Unconsolidated deposits** |        |       |      |      |
    | Sand              | 28.1   | 38.9  | 44.4 | 4.9  |
    | Dune              | c      | 42.1  | c    | 8.3  |
    | Silt              | 31.5   | 45.2  | 50.8 | 5.6  |
    | Clay              | 40.1   | 46.1  | 55   | 4.5  |
    | Glacial till      | 14.3   | 23.5  | 34   | c    |
    | Peat              | c      | 92    | c    | c    |

    Values in per cent. "c" means there were too few measurements to give a value. Field values for sand and gravel are usually lower, 25 to 35%, because the laboratory samples in this table were loosely packed.

  .container width:{50%}
    | Material          | Min    | Mean  | Max  | SD   |
    |-------------------|--------|-------|------|------|
    | **Sedimentary rocks**    |        |       |      |      |
    | Sandstone         | 4      | 20.4  | 28.6  | 8.6  |
    | Limestone         | 0.44   | 8.67  | 34.7 | 8.6  |
    | Dolostone         | 0.4    | 7.46  | 26   | c    |
    | Shale             | 0.75   | 10.8  | 27.2 | 8.3  |
    | **Igneous rocks**        |        |       |      |      |
    | Granite           | <0.001 |       | 0.01 |      |
    | Basalt            | <0.01  |       | 0.1  |      |

    Sandstone has lower porosity than sand because mineral cement fills part of the pore space. Burial and chemical change after deposition (diagenesis) reduce porosity further with age and depth. Unfractured igneous rock has almost no porosity, so in granite nearly all the water is in fractures like those in the figure two slides back.


# Effective porosity

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - Water cannot flow through every void. **Effective porosity** $ n_e $ is the fraction of the volume made up of voids that connect to each other, so that water can flow through the sample.
    - In a clean sand or gravel all the pores connect, and $ n_e \approx n_T $.
    - In a fractured rock they do not. The pores of a shale hold water but connect poorly, so the water that flows, flows in the fractures. The **fracture porosity** $ n_f $ is then the effective porosity.
    - Below the weathered zone near the surface, fractures are typically tens to hundreds of micrometres wide. Fractures 100 µm wide and one metre apart give

      $ n_f = \frac{10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m}}{1\,\mathrm{m}} = 10^{-4} $

    - Typical fracture porosities are $ 10^{-4} $ to $ 10^{-3} $. The total porosity of the same shale is about 0.10, which is up to a thousand times larger.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(78%)[Fractured Coconino Sandstone](../image/franklin_fig_3-3.png "Fractured Coconino Sandstone, northeastern Arizona. The fracturing runs both vertically and horizontally, and varies from place to place in one unit.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Schwartz & Zhang p. 45 gives $ n_f \approx 5\times10^{-2} $ for shale. That is a misprint: the book's own worked example uses $ 3\times10^{-3} $.


# Dual porosity

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    A fractured rock has **two** porosities, and each one has a different role.

    | | Matrix | Fractures |
    |---|---|---|
    | Porosity | 0.05 – 0.15 | $ 10^{-4} $ – $ 10^{-3} $ |
    | Holds the water | **yes** | no |
    | Carries the flow | no | **yes** |

    - The matrix (the rock between the fractures) holds almost all of the water. The fractures carry almost all of the flow.
    - A contaminant in the fractures slowly diffuses into the matrix. After clean water has replaced it in the fractures, it diffuses back out for decades. This is why contaminated fractured rock is so hard to clean up. We return to this later in the course.
    - Karst works in the same way, except that dissolution has widened the fractures into conduits.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Caverns and fractures](../image/internet_porosity_primarysecondary.png "Primary porosity between grains; secondary porosity along fractures and solution openings.")


# Same porosity, very different yield

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Two wells drilled to the same depth, in two materials with the **same porosity** of 0.30.

    | | Gravel | Silt |
    |---|---|---|
    | Porosity | 0.30 | 0.30 |
    | Water stored per m³ | 300 L | 300 L |
    | Hydraulic conductivity | $ 10^{2} $ m/day | $ 10^{-2} $ m/day |
    | Yield to a well | supplies a town | fills a bucket |

    Both materials hold the same amount of water. Only the gravel releases it to a well at a useful rate.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{The extreme case: clay} background:{.lightred}
      **Clay** has a mean porosity of 46%, among the highest in the porosity table. It also has the lowest hydraulic conductivity of the sediments in the conductivity figure later in this lecture. Clay is nearly half water by volume, but a well in clay yields almost nothing.

    - Porosity sets **storage**.
    - Conductivity sets **transmission**.
    - An aquifer needs both. A unit with high porosity and low conductivity is a **confining bed**. Next week's lecture covers aquifers and confining beds.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Occurrence: where the water sits


# Occurrence of Groundwater

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - Near the surface the pores hold **both water and air**. This is the **unsaturated zone**. Its water content rises when water infiltrates and falls when plants take it up or it evaporates. Plants get their water from this zone, and the soil moisture lecture covers it.
    - Deeper, the pores hold **only water**: the **saturated zone**, and the water in it is **groundwater**.
    - The **water table** separates the two. It is the surface on which water pressure equals atmospheric pressure.
    - Just above the water table, surface tension holds up a saturated layer at below-atmospheric pressure. This is the **capillary fringe**. By the definition above it is not groundwater. It is millimetres thick in gravel and metres thick in clay.
    - Water that passes below the roots and reaches the water table is **recharge**.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(62%)[Subsurface water](../image/franklin_fig_3-4.png "Precipitation infiltrates the soil zone and percolates through the unsaturated zone. What reaches the water table becomes groundwater.")


# Flow of Groundwater

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - Recharge arrives at the water table and then moves **down and sideways** towards a discharge point: a river, a spring, a wetland, the sea.
    - One quantity drives both the downward and the sideways motion: the gradient in hydraulic head. The next section defines head and shows why its gradient differs from the pressure gradient.
    - Surface runoff and groundwater discharge reach the same river, but they take very different times to get there.
    - Groundwater discharge is the baseflow from the runoff lecture. A river keeps flowing after months without rain because water that fell in earlier years is still reaching it.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(100%)[A groundwater flow system](../image/franklin_fig_3-5.png "Some precipitation becomes recharge; groundwater moves down and laterally and discharges into the nearby river.")

    .align{center}
      .container alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{5px}
        Surface runoff reaches the stream in hours. Groundwater takes years.


# How long does it take?

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Residence time is the length of the flow path divided by the speed of the water. The formula for the speed comes from Darcy's law, later in this lecture:

    $ t = \frac{L}{v} = \frac{L\, n_e}{K\, i} $

    | System | $ L $ | $ v $ | $ t $ |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Hillslope to creek, sand | 300 m | 0.15 m/day | 5 years |
    | Small basin, sand and gravel | 20 km | 0.5 m/day | 100 years |
    | Great Artesian Basin | 1000 km | 1 m/year | $ 10^{5} $ – $ 10^{6} $ years |

    - The water in a bore in western Queensland fell as rain on the Great Dividing Range before the last glacial period.
    - This is the same residence time as in the water cycle lecture, but calculated for one flow path instead of a whole reservoir.

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{Why this matters for management} background:{.lightred}
      A store with a residence time of $ 10^{5} $ years does not refill within any period that a water plan covers. Pumping it removes water for good, which is called **groundwater mining**. The management question is how fast to use it, because it will not recover.

    The groundwater management lecture returns to this, and Assignment III uses the same calculation.


