#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Hydraulic head: what sets the direction of flow


# Pressure does not set the direction of flow

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Take a column of water standing still in a tube. Pressure at depth $ d $ below the surface is

    $ P = \rho_w g d $

    - The pressure at the bottom is larger than the pressure at the top. If water always flowed from high to low pressure, it would flow upward.
    - But nothing moves. The column is in equilibrium.
    - The same is true below a water table. Pressure increases with depth everywhere, even where the water is not moving at all.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      **Pressure alone cannot tell you the direction of flow.** The quantity that can is hydraulic head.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 380 230" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs><marker id="ar" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
        <text x="110" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">pressure</text>
        <rect x="70" y="30" width="80" height="170" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="60"  x2="98"  y2="60"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="115" x2="120" y2="115" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <line x1="78" y1="170" x2="145" y2="170" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <text x="64" y="64"  font-size="11" text-anchor="end">small</text>
        <text x="64" y="119" font-size="11" text-anchor="end">larger</text>
        <text x="64" y="174" font-size="11" text-anchor="end">largest</text>
        <text x="300" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">head</text>
        <rect x="260" y="30" width="80" height="170" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <line x1="250" y1="30" x2="352" y2="30" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3"/>
        <line x1="275" y1="60"  x2="275" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="300" y1="115" x2="300" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="325" y1="170" x2="325" y2="32" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <circle cx="275" cy="60"  r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <circle cx="300" cy="115" r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <circle cx="325" cy="170" r="3" fill="#b43c3c"/>
        <text x="300" y="222" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">every point: the same h</text>
      </svg>

    Pressure changes from the top to the bottom of the column, but head stays the same. Where head is constant, there is no flow, however the pressure varies.


# Hydraulic head

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    The energy of a unit weight of water at a point has three parts. This is the Bernoulli equation, written in units of length:

    $ h = z + \frac{P}{\rho_w g} + \frac{v^2}{2g} $

    - $ z $: **elevation head**, the height of the point above the datum, usually sea level. This is the gravitational part.
    - $ P/\rho_w g $: **pressure head**, the height of the water column the pressure would support.
    - $ v^2/2g $: **velocity head**, the kinetic part.

    Groundwater moves at about 1 m/day, which is $ 1.2 \times 10^{-5} $ m/s, so

    $ \frac{v^2}{2g} = \frac{(1.2\times10^{-5})^2}{19.6} \approx 7\times10^{-12}\ \mathrm{m} $

    compared with head differences of metres. We therefore ignore the velocity head:

    $ h = z + \frac{P}{\rho_w g} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(90%)[Head components](../image/franklin_fig_3-9.png "Elevation head, pressure head and total head at a point in the flow field.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Going down the still column, the pressure head increases by exactly as much as the elevation head $ z $ decreases, so their sum stays the same.


# Measuring head in the field

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    The water level in a **standpipe** that is open at the bottom gives the head at the open end.

    - In principle (a): read the elevation of the water level against a scale. In the figure it is 110 m above sea level.
    - In practice (b): the pipe has no scale on it, so head is calculated from two measurements.
      - Survey or high-precision GPS gives the **elevation of the top of casing**, $ EL_{TOC} $ = 160 m.
      - A weighted electric tape gives the **depth to water**, $ DTW $ = 50 m.

      $ h = EL_{TOC} - DTW = 160 - 50 = 110\ \mathrm{m} $

    - This is the head at the **measurement point**, the middle of the screen at the bottom of the pipe. It is not the head at the water table.
    - The screen is a slotted section of casing that lets water in. Its open area lets the water level in the pipe follow changes in the ground, which matters most in low-conductivity ground.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(80%)[Measuring head](../image/franklin_fig_3-8.png "(a) Head as the elevation of the water column, 110 m asl, at the red measurement point. (b) The same head assembled from the casing elevation and the depth to water.")


# One site, two heads

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 330 220" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="0" y="45" width="330" height="175" fill="#f2ead9"/>
        <line x1="0" y1="45" x2="330" y2="45" stroke="#6b5b3e" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="70" y="30" width="16" height="160" fill="#cfcfcf" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="70" y="150" width="16" height="40" fill="#e8c96a" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="200" y="30" width="12" height="80" fill="#cfcfcf" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="200" y="95" width="12" height="15" fill="#e8c96a" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="70" y1="140" x2="86" y2="140" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="4"/>
        <line x1="200" y1="62" x2="212" y2="62" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-width="4"/>
        <text x="60" y="26" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">water well</text>
        <text x="206" y="26" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">piezometer</text>
        <text x="96" y="143" font-size="11" fill="#1f6fb2">h = 73 m</text>
        <text x="222" y="65" font-size="11" fill="#1f6fb2">h = 84 m</text>
        <line x1="0" y1="72" x2="330" y2="78" stroke="#1f6fb2" stroke-dasharray="5,4" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="240" y="92" font-size="9" fill="#1f6fb2">water table 81.5 m</text>
        <line x1="150" y1="120" x2="150" y2="165" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#a2)"/>
        <text x="156" y="148" font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">flow</text>
        <defs><marker id="a2" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

  .container width:{55%}
    Two pipes at one site, screened at different depths. The shallow one reads **84 m**. The deep one reads **73 m**.

    - Both readings are correct. This site has no single "groundwater level".
    - Head is 11 m lower at depth, so the hydraulic gradient points **downward**. Water flows from the shallow unit into the deep aquifer.
    - Head belongs to a **point**. A head measurement has no meaning until you state the depth of the screen and the unit it is in.
    - This is why piezometers are installed in **nests**: three short screens at three depths at one location, to measure how head changes with depth.
    - A well with a long screen gives an average head over the full length of the screen. At a site like this, it cannot show the vertical gradient.


# Three types of well

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Water wells**: wide casing, long screen. Many thousands already exist, so they are the only practical source of data for regional studies. They work well in a single aquifer where flow is horizontal. There, lines of equal head (**equipotentials**) are vertical, so the depth of the screen does not change the reading.
    - **Piezometers**: narrow casing, short screen, sealed above with bentonite clay. They measure head at a point. They are used at contaminated sites, for dewatering, and in nests to measure vertical gradients.
    - **Water table observation wells**: a long screen that covers the full range of water table depths, so the water table is always somewhere in the screen.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The observation well differs from the other two. Its measurement point is **not** the middle of the screen. It is the water table, which moves up and down through the year. Pressure head at the water table is zero, so head there equals the elevation of the water table.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Three devices](../image/franklin_fig_3-17.png "A water well, a water table observation well and a nest of three piezometers at one site. Heads: 73 m in the aquifer, 81.5 m at the water table, and 84, 81 and 78 m in the piezometer nest. Dashed lines are equipotentials; red arrows are flow directions.")

    In the figure the nest reads 84, 81 and 78 m from shallow to deep. Head decreases with depth, so flow in the shallow unit is downward.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Darcy's law


# Darcy's experiment

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - **Henry Darcy**, a civil engineer, built the water supply of Dijon in the 1850s. He needed to know how large to make the sand filters.
    - His apparatus was a cylinder of cross-section area $ A $, packed with sand. Water flowed through it at a measured rate $ Q $. Two manometers a distance $ \Delta l $ apart measured the heads $ h_1 $ and $ h_2 $.
    - He changed the flow rate, the head difference, the length of the column and the type of sand, and found one relationship:

      $ \frac{Q}{A} = -K \frac{dh}{dl} $

    - $ K $, the **hydraulic conductivity**, includes all the properties of the sand. Darcy measured $ K $. He did not derive it. A derivation came about a century later, and we do it later in this section.
    - Darcy's law is an **empirical** law, like Ohm's law, and it has the same form: a flux proportional to the gradient of a potential.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Darcy's apparatus](../image/franklin_fig_3-7.png "Laboratory apparatus demonstrating Darcy's equation: flow Q through a column of length Δl, with head h₁ and h₂ read from two manometers.")


# Darcy's law, and its sign

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    $ q = -K \frac{dh}{dl} \qquad Q = qA = -KA \frac{dh}{dl} $

    - $ q = Q/A $ is the **specific discharge**, also called the **Darcy velocity**. It is the volume of water per unit time through a unit area of the whole column face, in m/day.
    - $ dh/dl $ is the change in head **per metre along the direction $ l $**. The minus sign makes water flow in the direction in which head decreases.
    - You can also define the gradient as a positive head loss over a positive distance:

      $ i = \frac{h_1 - h_2}{\Delta l} \quad (h_1 > h_2) $

      Then $ q = Ki $, and the flow is from point 1 towards point 2. Both forms are in use. Choose one and use it consistently.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      The textbook's equation 3.5 uses $ i = (h_1-h_2)/\Delta l $ **and** $ q = -Ki $ together. That gives flow from low head to high head, which is wrong, because $ (h_1-h_2)/\Delta l = -dh/dl $.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(78%)[Head gradient in a column](../image/franklin_fig_3-6.png "A dipping aquifer with head measured at two points: h₁ = 145 m, h₂ = 125 m, Δl = 5000 m, so i = 0.004.")

    The gradient has **no units**: here the head falls by four millimetres for each metre of distance.


# Darcy velocity and pore velocity

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - $ q $ is the discharge divided by the area of the **whole** column face, including the solid grains. No water actually travels at speed $ q $.
    - Water flows only through the connected voids, which make up a fraction $ n_e $ of the face. The **average linear velocity**, or pore velocity, is therefore

      $ v = \frac{q}{n_e} = -\frac{K}{n_e}\frac{dh}{dl} $

    - Because $ n_e < 1 $, $ v $ is always larger than $ q $.
    - Use $ v $ to calculate travel times, for example how long a contaminant takes to reach a bore.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      $ v $ is an **average**. Water moves at very different speeds through wide and narrow pores, and this makes a contaminant plume spread out along its flow path. The spreading is called **hydrodynamic dispersion**, and we cover it later in the course.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(100%)[Pore velocity](../image/internet_pore_velocity_schematic.jpg "Flow takes place only in the connected pore space, so the true speed exceeds the Darcy velocity.")

    .align{center}
      .container
        [gw-project.org](https://books.gw-project.org/hydrogeologic-properties-of-earth-materials-and-principles-of-groundwater-flow/chapter/darcys-law/)


# Worked example: sand and fractured shale

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Use $ K = 1\times10^{-4} $ m/s and $ i = 0.004 $, the gradient of the dipping aquifer two slides back. How fast does the water travel?

    **(a) Granular sand,** $ n_e \approx n_T = 0.30 $:

    $ v = \frac{Ki}{n_e} = \frac{10^{-4}\times 0.004}{0.30} = 1.33\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m/s} $

    $ v = 0.115\ \mathrm{m/day} $

    **(b) Fractured shale,** same $ K $, $ n_T = 0.10 $, $ n_f = 3\times10^{-3} $:

    $ v = \frac{10^{-4}\times 0.004}{3\times10^{-3}} = 1.33\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m/s} $

    $ v = 11.5\ \mathrm{m/day} $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{What the result means} background:{.lightred}
      Both rocks have the same conductivity, the same gradient and the same discharge $ q $. In the shale the water travels **one hundred times** faster, because the same flow passes through one hundredth of the area.

    - A spill above the fractured shale reaches a bore 1 km away in **three months**. Above the sand it takes **24 years**.
    - This is why effective porosity is worth measuring. The misprinted value $ n_f = 0.05 $ would give 0.7 m/day, a wrong answer that makes the site look safer than it is.

    Note: the textbook prints 0.152 m/day as the answer to (a). Its own numbers give 0.115 m/day.


# Where Darcy's law comes from

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Darcy measured $ K $. We can derive how $ K $ depends on grain size if we treat the pore space as a bundle of narrow tubes.

    For laminar flow in a tube of diameter $ d $, Poiseuille's law gives the mean velocity caused by a pressure gradient:

    $ \bar{v} = \frac{d^2}{32\mu}\left(-\frac{dP}{dx}\right) $

    Write the pressure in terms of head, $ P = \rho_w g (h-z) $. For a horizontal tube, $ -dP/dx = -\rho_w g\, dh/dx $:

    $ \bar{v} = \frac{\rho_w g\, d^2}{32\mu}\left(-\frac{dh}{dx}\right) $

    The specific discharge is $ q = n_e \bar{v} $, divided by a factor for how winding the paths are (the tortuosity). All the dimensionless factors combine into one constant $ c $:

    $ q = \underbrace{\frac{c\,\rho_w g\, d^2}{\mu}}_{K}\left(-\frac{dh}{dx}\right) $

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{Three results from the derivation} background:{.lightblue}
      1. **$ q $ is proportional to the gradient**, as Darcy observed. This is true only while the flow in the tubes is laminar.
      2. **$ K $ has two factors**: $ c\,d^2 $ depends on the rock, and $ \rho_w g/\mu $ depends on the fluid. The rock part is the intrinsic permeability, later in this lecture.
      3. **$ K $ increases with the square of the pore diameter**, and pore diameter increases with grain diameter. Every empirical formula for $ K $ in the next section is a form of $ K \propto d^2 $.

    Grains ten times larger give a conductivity one hundred times larger. This explains most of the range of $ K $ from gravel to clay.


# Where Darcy's law fails

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    **Too fast: turbulence.** The derivation assumed laminar flow. To test this, calculate a Reynolds number from the grain diameter:

    $ Re = \frac{\rho_w q d}{\mu} $

    and the law holds for $ Re $ below about 1 to 10.

    | Setting | $ q $ | $ d $ | $ Re $ |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Sand aquifer | 1 m/day | 1 mm | 0.01 |
    | Coarse gravel | 100 m/day | 10 mm | 12 |
    | Karst conduit, near a pumped well | much larger | much larger | $ \gg 10 $ |

    At higher $ Re $, discharge increases more slowly than the gradient, so Darcy's law predicts a drawdown that is too small.

  .container width:{50%}
    **Too slow: a threshold.** In clays with very low permeability, charged mineral surfaces hold the nearby water tightly. The water may not move at all until the gradient is larger than a minimum value (Bolt and Groenevelt, 1969).

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both failures happen only at the extremes. In nearly every aquifer, Darcy's law is accurate enough, and the theory lecture builds on it.

    This course covers two cases where the law fails in practice: **karst conduits**, in the aquifers lecture, and the area **directly around a pumping well**, in the well hydraulics lecture.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Conductivity, permeability, and how to estimate them


# Hydraulic conductivity: twelve orders of magnitude

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    !(88%)[Conductivity of aquifer materials](../image/franklin_fig_3-11.png "Hydraulic conductivity for selected aquifer materials, and their potential as an aquifer.")

  .container width:{55%}
    - $ K $ has units of length over time. Values range from $ 10^{-8} $ m/day in unfractured igneous rock to $ 10^{4} $ m/day in limestone with caverns.
    - Few other properties in the earth sciences vary over twelve orders of magnitude. The porosity of most sediments and sedimentary rocks, for comparison, stays between about 1% and 50%.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      **What this means for a site investigation.** An error of a factor of two in the gradient gives an error of a factor of two in the flow. An error in the type of material can give an error of a factor of $ 10^{6} $. So most of the budget goes into finding out what the ground is made of.

    - In the figure, fractured rock of each type has a conductivity 10 to 1000 times higher than the same rock without fractures.


# Intrinsic permeability

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    The derivation split $ K $ into a rock part and a fluid part. The rock part is $ k $:

    $ q = -\frac{k\rho_w g}{\mu}\frac{dh}{dl} \qquad K = \frac{k\rho_w g}{\mu} $

    - $ k $ is the **intrinsic permeability**, with units of **area**: $ \mathrm{m^2} $, or $ \mathrm{cm^2} $, or darcy, where $ 1\ \mathrm{darcy} = 9.87\times10^{-13}\ \mathrm{m^2} $.
    - It is an area because it is the $ c\,d^2 $ from the derivation, which is proportional to the cross-section of a pore.
    - At 20 °C and 1 atm, with $ \rho_w = 998.2 $ kg/m³ and $ \mu = 1.002\times10^{-3} $ kg/(m s):

      $ K = 9.77\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{m^{-1}s^{-1}} \cdot k $

      $ k = 1.023\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{m\,s} \cdot K $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Why use k instead of K?} background:{.lightblue}
      $ K $ describes the rock **and** the water together. If the fluid changes, $ K $ changes, even though the rock is the same. Petroleum, contaminant and geothermal studies deal with fluids other than cold fresh water, so they use $ k $.

    **Temperature changes $ K $.** Over natural groundwater temperatures, only the viscosity $ \mu $ changes noticeably:

    | | $ \mu $ (kg m⁻¹s⁻¹) | $ K $ relative |
    |---|---|---|
    | 5 °C | $ 1.52\times10^{-3} $ | 1.00 |
    | 25 °C | $ 0.89\times10^{-3} $ | 1.71 |

    The same rock has a conductivity **1.7 times higher** at 25 °C than at 5 °C, although the rock itself has not changed.


# Estimating K from grain size

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    The derivation gave $ K \propto d^2 $. Each empirical formula uses this result, with a constant fitted to data and its own choice of grain diameter $ d $.

    | Source | Equation |
    |---|---|
    | Hazen (1911) | $ K = C d_{10}^2 $, C = 100–150 cm⁻¹s⁻¹ |
    | Harleman (1963) | $ k = 6.54\times10^{-4} d_{10}^2 $ |
    | Kozeny–Carman | $ K = \frac{\rho_w g}{\mu}\frac{n^3}{(1-n)^2}\frac{d_m^2}{180} $ |

    - $ d_{10} $ is the **effective grain size**: the sieve opening that lets through 10% of the sample by weight. 90% of the sample is coarser.
    - Only Kozeny–Carman includes porosity, through $ n^3/(1-n)^2 $, and it shows the fluid factor $ \rho_w g/\mu $ explicitly. It is the bundle-of-tubes derivation with a more careful treatment of the pore shape.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Why the tenth percentile and not the mean?} background:{.lightred}
      Because the smallest grains (the **fines**) control the flow. In a sand with 10% clay, the clay particles fill the narrow openings between the sand grains. These openings control the resistance to flow, so the clay lowers $ K $ much more than its share of the volume suggests. $ d_{10} $ takes this into account.

      The same idea returns later in this lecture, in the vertical conductivity of layered ground. There too, the least conductive material controls the result.

    Example from the tutorial: $ d_{10} = 0.095 $ mm gives $ K \approx 6\times10^{-3} $ cm/s from Harleman and $ 9\times10^{-3} $ cm/s from Hazen. Grain-size estimates rarely agree better than this factor of 1.5. Field tests give more reliable values.


# Measuring K in the laboratory: constant head

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    Used for **coarse** material, $ K > 10^{-3} $ cm/s.

    - Hold the head difference $ h $ fixed across a core of length $ L $ and cross-section area $ A $.
    - When the flow is steady, measure the outflow rate $ Q $ by collecting water for a known time.
    - Rearrange Darcy's law to get

      $ K = \frac{QL}{Ah} $

    - The overflow at the top keeps the supply head constant. Without it, the head falls as the reservoir drains, and the flow is no longer steady.
    - This is the same set-up as Darcy's experiment.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(58%)[Constant head](../image/franklin_fig_3-13-a.png "Constant-head permeameter: a fixed head difference Δh across a core of length ℓ.")


# Measuring K in the laboratory: falling head

.row
  .container width:{60%}
    Used for **fine** material: fine sands through to clays.

    - There is no constant supply. Water in a standpipe of cross-section area $ a $ drains through the core, and the head falls from $ h_0 $ to $ h_1 $ in time $ t_1 - t_0 $:

      $ K = 2.3\,\frac{aL}{A(t_1-t_0)}\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{h_0}{h_1}\right) $

    - The choice of test is practical. In a clay, the flow $ Q $ in a constant-head test is too small to measure, but a falling water level is easy to measure. The standpipe is narrow ($ a $ is much smaller than $ A $), so a small volume of flow gives a large drop in level.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Both tests measure a core a few centimetres across, which the coring has disturbed. The next section asks whether this value describes the whole aquifer.

  .container width:{40%}
    !(44%)[Falling head](../image/franklin_fig_3-13-b.png "Falling-head permeameter: the level in the standpipe falls from h₀ to h₁.")


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Real ground: heterogeneous and anisotropic


# Heterogeneity and anisotropy

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 340 250" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <text x="95" y="14" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">isotropic</text>
        <text x="255" y="14" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">anisotropic</text>
        <text x="8"  y="70" font-size="11" transform="rotate(-90 8,70)" text-anchor="middle">homogeneous</text>
        <text x="8"  y="190" font-size="11" transform="rotate(-90 8,190)" text-anchor="middle">heterogeneous</text>
        <rect x="25" y="25" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <circle cx="95" cy="70" r="26" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="185" y="25" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="45" x2="325" y2="45" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="70" x2="325" y2="70" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="95" x2="325" y2="95" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <ellipse cx="255" cy="70" rx="40" ry="16" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="25" y="145" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <circle cx="60" cy="175" r="16" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <circle cx="125" cy="205" r="26" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <rect x="185" y="145" width="140" height="90" fill="#e8dcc0" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="165" x2="325" y2="171" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <line x1="185" y1="195" x2="325" y2="201" stroke="#a08a5e"/>
        <ellipse cx="225" cy="180" rx="26" ry="10" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
        <ellipse cx="288" cy="212" rx="34" ry="13" fill="none" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="2"/>
      </svg>

    Each red outline shows how far water travels from a point in one unit of time. A circle means that $ K $ is the same in every direction. An ellipse means that it is not.

  .container width:{55%}
    - **Homogeneous or heterogeneous** asks whether $ K $ changes from **place to place**.
    - **Isotropic or anisotropic** asks whether $ K $ changes with **direction** at one place.
    - The two properties are independent, and all four combinations occur. Real ground is usually heterogeneous and anisotropic, as in the bottom right box.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The main point of this section: **heterogeneity at a small scale becomes anisotropy at a larger scale.** A stack of layers, each of them isotropic, behaves as one anisotropic material when you look at the stack as a whole.


# K varies within a single unit

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Thirty-two measurements of $ K $, all from **one** geological unit:

    | | value (m/s) |
    |---|---|
    | smallest | $ 2\times10^{-10} $ |
    | largest | $ 1.5\times10^{-6} $ |
    | arithmetic mean $ \eta $ | $ 2.64\times10^{-7} $ |
    | geometric mean $ G $ | $ 9.93\times10^{-8} $ |
    | harmonic mean $ H $ | $ 5.72\times10^{-9} $ |

    - The values span four orders of magnitude, in one unit that a geological map shows as a single colour.
    - The three means differ by a factor of **46**. If you give "the" conductivity of a unit, you must say which mean you used.

  .container width:{50%}
    - A histogram of $ K $ is strongly skewed, but a histogram of $ \log K $ is close to a normal distribution. $ K $ is **log-normally distributed**. In this sample the mean of $ \log K $ is $ -7.0 $ and its standard deviation is $ 0.76 $.
    - This is why hydrogeologists use geometric means and plot conductivity on logarithmic axes.

    .box title:{Which mean is the right one?} background:{.lightblue}
      It depends on the direction of flow relative to the layers. The next two slides show why:

      - flow **along** the layers: arithmetic mean
      - flow **across** the layers: harmonic mean
      - any other case lies between the two (Matheron, 1967). If $ K $ varies in the same way in every direction, the geometric mean is the usual estimate.


# Layers in parallel: the arithmetic mean

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Flow **along** the bedding. Each layer has the same head drop over the same distance, so each has the same gradient $ i $. The total discharge is the sum of the discharges in the layers:

    $ Q = \sum_i K_{hi} b_i i \;=\; K_h \left(\sum_i b_i\right) i $

    $ K_h = \frac{\sum_i b_i K_{hi}}{\sum_i b_i} $

    a **thickness-weighted arithmetic mean**.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      The most conductive layer controls $ K_h $. In one metre of gravel within a hundred metres of clay, the gravel carries nearly all of the horizontal flow. The clay has almost no effect on $ K_h $.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="40" y="25"  width="240" height="26" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="51"  width="240" height="16" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="67"  width="240" height="34" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="101" width="240" height="20" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="121" width="240" height="30" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <text x="292" y="42"  font-size="10">K₁ b₁</text>
        <text x="292" y="63"  font-size="10">K₂ b₂</text>
        <text x="292" y="88"  font-size="10">K₃ b₃</text>
        <line x1="55" y1="38"  x2="265" y2="38"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="59"  x2="120" y2="59"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="84"  x2="265" y2="84"  stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="111" x2="120" y2="111" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <line x1="55" y1="136" x2="265" y2="136" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a3)"/>
        <text x="160" y="175" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">same gradient in every layer, discharges add</text>
        <defs><marker id="a3" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

    Each layer carries $ K_{hi} b_i i $. Thick, conductive layers carry almost all of the flow.


# Layers in series: the harmonic mean

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Flow **across** the bedding. Now the same $ q $ passes through every layer. The total head loss is the sum of the head losses in the layers:

    $ \Delta h = \sum_i q\,\frac{b_i}{K_{vi}} \;=\; \frac{q}{K_v}\sum_i b_i $

    $ K_v = \frac{\sum_i b_i}{\sum_i b_i/K_{vi}} $

    a **harmonic mean**.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      Now the least conductive layer controls $ K_v $. One metre of clay within a hundred metres of gravel sets the vertical conductivity of the whole stack almost on its own, because the water cannot go around it.

    Electrical resistors behave in the same way. In parallel, the path with the lowest resistance carries most of the current. In series, the largest resistance controls the total.

  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="40" y="25"  width="240" height="26" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="51"  width="240" height="16" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="67"  width="240" height="34" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="101" width="240" height="20" fill="#9aa6a0" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="40" y="121" width="240" height="30" fill="#e3d5a8" stroke="#333"/>
        <line x1="110" y1="20" x2="110" y2="158" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a4)"/>
        <line x1="210" y1="20" x2="210" y2="158" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#a4)"/>
        <text x="292" y="63"  font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">tight</text>
        <text x="292" y="115" font-size="10" fill="#b43c3c">tight</text>
        <text x="160" y="180" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">same q through every layer, head losses add</text>
        <defs><marker id="a4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#b43c3c"/></marker></defs>
      </svg>

    The two grey layers make up a fifth of the thickness but cause nearly all of the head loss. A piezometer nest through this stack would show almost constant head in the sandy layers, and a steep drop in head across the grey ones.


# Worked example: layering makes anisotropy

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    A 300 m sequence of interbedded sandstone and shale, 75% sandstone. **Both rocks are isotropic.**

    - Sandstone: $ K = 1\times10^{-5} $ m/s, 225 m in total
    - Shale: $ K = 1.92\times10^{-12} $ m/s, 75 m in total

    $ K_h = \frac{225(10^{-5}) + 75(1.92\times10^{-12})}{300} = 7.5\times10^{-6} $ m/s

    $ K_v = \frac{300}{\frac{225}{10^{-5}} + \frac{75}{1.92\times10^{-12}}} = 7.7\times10^{-12} $ m/s

    $ \frac{K_h}{K_v} \approx 10^{6} $

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{What the two answers mean} background:{.lightblue}
      $ K_h $ is three quarters of the sandstone value, the sandstone's share of the thickness. The shale has almost no effect.

      $ K_v $ is four times the shale value, because the shale is a quarter of the thickness. The sandstone has almost no effect.

    - Neither rock is anisotropic. The factor of a million comes **only from the layering**.
    - So you cannot say whether a rock is anisotropic without saying at what scale you look at it.
    - This is why regional groundwater models with 500 m cells use $ K_h/K_v $ ratios of $ 10^2 $ to $ 10^4 $: each cell contains many layers.


# Anisotropy at two scales

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    ### Measured on a core

    | Material | Horizontal $ K $ (m/s) | Vertical $ K $ (m/s) | Ratio |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Anhydrite | $ 10^{-14} $–$ 10^{-12} $ | $ 10^{-15} $–$ 10^{-13} $ | 10 |
    | Chalk | $ 10^{-10} $–$ 10^{-8} $ | $ 5\times10^{-11} $–$ 5\times10^{-9} $ | 2 |
    | Limestone, dolomite | $ 10^{-9} $–$ 10^{-7} $ | $ 5\times10^{-10} $–$ 5\times10^{-8} $ | 2 |
    | Sandstone | $ 5\times10^{-13} $–$ 10^{-10} $ | $ 2.5\times10^{-13} $–$ 5\times10^{-11} $ | 2 |
    | Shale | $ 10^{-14} $–$ 10^{-12} $ | $ 10^{-15} $–$ 10^{-13} $ | 10 |
    | Salt | $ 10^{-14} $ | $ 10^{-14} $ | 1 |

  .container width:{45%}
    - A core samples **one bed**. At that scale, anisotropy comes from flat grains that lined up when they were deposited, and the ratio is about **2 to 10**.
    - The much larger ratios in regional models come from **layering**, as calculated on the previous slide.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{.lightred} padding:{4px}
      The word "anisotropy" covers two different causes at two different scales. When you give a ratio, say which one you mean.

    - In both cases, water flows easily along a sedimentary sequence and with great difficulty across it. This is what makes confined aquifers possible.


# Darcy's law when direction matters

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    In an anisotropic material, $ \mathbf{q} $ and the head gradient usually **point in different directions**. $ K $ is then a tensor instead of a single number:

    $ \mathbf{q} = -\mathbf{K}\nabla h $

      $ \mathbf{K} = \begin{bmatrix} K_{xx} & K_{xy} & K_{xz} \\ K_{yx} & K_{yy} & K_{yz} \\ K_{zx} & K_{zy} & K_{zz} \end{bmatrix} $

    Each component of the flow depends on all three components of the gradient:

    $ q_x = -K_{xx}\frac{\partial h}{\partial x} - K_{xy}\frac{\partial h}{\partial y} - K_{xz}\frac{\partial h}{\partial z} $

    - The tensor is symmetric, $ K_{xy} = K_{yx} $, so six numbers describe it.
    - If you choose the coordinate axes along the **principal directions of anisotropy**, the off-diagonal terms are zero. For layered rock, these directions are along and across the bedding:

      $ q_x = -K_{xx}\frac{\partial h}{\partial x},\quad q_y = -K_{yy}\frac{\partial h}{\partial y},\quad q_z = -K_{zz}\frac{\partial h}{\partial z} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Anisotropy from layering](../image/internet_anisotropy.png "The same layered material in three orientations relative to the axes. Here σ is electrical conductivity, and hydraulic conductivity behaves in exactly the same way: the tensor components swap as the layering is reoriented, although the material is unchanged.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      In anisotropic ground, **flow lines do not cross equipotential lines at right angles**. Right angles are correct only in isotropic ground, but almost every map you will see assumes them.


# Flow lines bend at a boundary

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    Two conditions hold where flow crosses from unit 1 into unit 2. The angle $ \alpha $ is measured from the line **perpendicular** to the boundary:

    - Mass is conserved, so the component of $ q $ **across** the boundary is continuous:

      $ q_1\cos\alpha_1 = q_2\cos\alpha_2 $

    - Head is continuous across the boundary, so the head gradient **along** it is the same on both sides. With $ q = -K\,dh/dl $, this gives $ q_1\sin\alpha_1/K_1 = q_2\sin\alpha_2/K_2 $.

    Divide the second equation by the first to get the **tangent law of refraction**:

    $ \frac{K_1}{K_2} = \frac{\tan\alpha_1}{\tan\alpha_2} $

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Refraction of flow lines](../image/franklin_fig_3-15.png "Flow line refraction at boundaries between materials of different permeability: in (a) k₂ > k₁, in (b) k₂ < k₁, and in panel c, k₂ ≫ k₁.")

    Two limiting cases are easy to remember:

    - Into a **much less conductive** unit: $ \alpha_2 \to 0 $. The flow turns to cross the unit **at right angles** (straight down for a horizontal layer).
    - Into a **much more conductive** unit: $ \alpha_2 \to 90° $. The flow turns to run **along** the unit.


# What refraction means in the field

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    **Into the shale.** Use the layered example again. Water in the sandstone that meets the boundary at 45° enters the shale at

    $ \alpha_2 = \arctan\!\left(\frac{1.92\times10^{-12}}{10^{-5}}\tan 45°\right) \approx 0° $

    Whatever the direction of flow in the sandstone, the water crosses the shale **vertically**. This is why cross sections show vertical arrows through every confining bed, and why flow through a confining bed can be treated as one-dimensional.

  .container width:{50%}
    **Into the sandstone.** In the opposite case, the flow turns until it is nearly parallel to the bedding. A thin permeable bed therefore collects flow from the layers above and below it, and carries it sideways.

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Together, these two results explain flow in layered ground. Water flows sideways in the conductive units and vertically through the less conductive units between them. Next week's lecture covers these units in more detail.

    The cross section at the end of this lecture shows both results: the flow arrows bend as they cross the contact.


#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Mapping a flow system


# Potentiometric surface maps

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A **potentiometric surface** is an imaginary surface formed by the levels to which water rises in wells screened in one aquifer (Meinzer, 1923). A map of this surface is a contour map of hydraulic head.
    - Two conditions must hold for the map to be meaningful.
      1. **One aquifer.** A deeper or shallower unit at the same place can have a very different head, as the piezometer nest showed. You must know which unit each well is screened in.
      2. **Horizontal flow in the aquifer.** Then equipotential lines are vertical and head does not change with depth. The depth and length of a well's screen then do not affect the reading.
    - **Contour spacing shows the gradient.** The head difference between contours is fixed, so close contours mean a steep gradient and widely spaced contours mean a gentle one.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[Making a potentiometric map](../image/franklin_fig_3-18.png "Three steps: in (a) plot the wells with their heads; in (b) contour to get equipotential lines and add flow directions; in panel c, add flow lines to make a flow net.")

    Three steps: plot the measured heads, draw contours, then add arrows at right angles to the contours, pointing towards lower head.


# Three warnings about these maps

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{A potentiometric surface is not a water table} background:{.lightred}
      For an **unconfined** aquifer the two are the same, because the top of the saturated zone is the water table.

      For a **confined** aquifer the potentiometric surface is **above** the top of the aquifer, often far above. It shows the level to which water would rise in a well. There is no water at that level in the ground.

      Where the potentiometric surface is above the ground surface, a well flows without pumping. This is an **artesian** bore, and the Great Artesian Basin is named after them.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Right angles are an assumption} background:{.lightred}
      Flow arrows are drawn at right angles to the contours. This is correct only in **isotropic** material.

      In layered ground, which is most ground, the flow direction turns away from the right angle, towards the direction with higher conductivity.

    - A third, practical warning: all the measurements on one map must be taken **at the same time**. Heads change by metres from season to season. A map made from readings spread over a year mixes seasonal changes with differences between places.


# Example: the Memphis aquifer

.row
  .container width:{45%}
    - The Memphis Sand in 1995, contoured from wells screened only in that unit.
    - Each well field produced a local **cone of depression**. The cones overlapped and merged into one regional low in the centre of the map.
    - Around the low the contours are close together, so the gradient is steep, and all flow arrows point inward. Most of the water that once flowed to natural outlets now flows to the pumps.
    - Pumping was later reduced, and the water levels stopped falling. The hydrograph on the next slide shows this.

  .container width:{55%}
    !(84%)[Memphis potentiometric surface](../image/franklin_fig_3-19.png "Potentiometric surface of the Memphis aquifer in 1995, contour interval 10 ft, hachured where the contours enclose a depression. Red dots are the wells used as control.")


# Water-level hydrographs

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A **water-level hydrograph** is a plot of head at one well against time. A map shows many places at one time. A hydrograph shows one place over a long time.
    - In the past, someone measured each well monthly with a tape. Today, loggers record the level and send it over satellite or mobile networks.
    - Well Sh:P-76 in the Memphis aquifer was monitored from 1925 to 1995. Its level falls steadily for forty years, then stays level from about 1970, when pumping was reduced.
    - What to look for in a hydrograph:
      - **Seasonal cycle**: recharge in the wet season, drawdown from irrigation pumping in the dry season.
      - **Trend**: the long-term balance between recharge and pumping.
      - **Steps**: a new well field, a new allocation rule, a drought.
    - Maps of past conditions are usually impossible to make, because nobody measured enough wells at the same time. Often a long record at one well is the only data available.

  .container width:{45%}
    !(100%)[A long hydrograph](../image/franklin_fig_3-20.png "Long-term hydrograph of well Sh:P-76, completed in the Memphis aquifer: water levels decline for about forty years and then stabilise.")


# Hydrogeological cross sections

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - A cross section is a vertical slice through the flow system. It is drawn **along** the main flow direction, so that little flow crosses the plane of the section.
    - It shows the rock layers, the conductivity of each unit, the heads measured in piezometer nests along the line, and the equipotentials drawn from them.
    - In the example, a low-$ K $ unit lies above an aquifer. In the upper unit, flow is nearly vertical and the equipotentials are close together, so the gradient is steep. In the aquifer, the equipotentials are vertical, and flow is horizontal with a gentle gradient. At the contact, the flow lines **refract**.
    - The vertical scale is exaggerated to make the layers visible. On an exaggerated section, flow lines and equipotentials do not meet at right angles, although they are often drawn that way.

  .container width:{50%}
    !(88%)[Cross section](../image/franklin_fig_3-21.png "Head distribution in a two-layer system from four piezometer nests: downward flow through the upper low-conductivity unit, horizontal flow in the aquifer, refraction at the contact.")

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      This figure brings together the main ideas of this lecture: heads measured at points, a gradient, two conductivities, refraction, and the flow direction that results from them.


