#

.row gap:{1cm} alignment:{center}
    .container width:{75%} alignment:{center} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.6cm} radius:{0.5cm} background:{.lightblue}

        ## Measuring groundwater storage from space


# Ground measurements sample small volumes

.row
  .container width:{55%}
    - A piezometer samples a volume a few metres across. A pumping test samples a few hundred metres. A basin is hundreds of kilometres across.
    - Head is not a volume of water. To convert a change in head to a change in **volume**, you need the storage properties from next week's lecture, and these are rarely known well.
    - Bores are not spread evenly over an aquifer. They are where someone needed water and paid for drilling: near towns, irrigation and roads.
    - So adding up bore records cannot answer the basin-scale question: **did this basin gain or lose water this decade?**

    .container width:{100%} borderwidth:{1px} background:{yellow} padding:{4px}
      Since 2002, satellites have answered this question directly. Water has mass, so a change in stored water changes the gravity field, and satellites can measure that change.

  .container width:{45%}
    .box title:{The size of the signal} background:{.lightblue}
      The Millennium Drought removed about **140 km³** of water from the Murray-Darling Basin.

      Spread over the basin's $ 1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $ that is a layer

      $ \frac{140\ \mathrm{km^3}}{1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2}} = 0.13\ \mathrm{m} $

      GRACE detects this 13 cm change from an altitude of about 490 km.


# How GRACE measures water mass

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    .html
      <svg viewBox="0 0 340 210" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <defs><marker id="a5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
          <path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 z" fill="#333"/></marker></defs>
        <path d="M10,150 Q90,120 170,128 Q250,136 330,120 L330,200 L10,200 Z" fill="#c8e1c8" stroke="#6b8f6b"/>
        <ellipse cx="170" cy="155" rx="70" ry="20" fill="#add8e6" stroke="#1f6fb2"/>
        <text x="170" y="159" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">extra water mass</text>
        <line x1="120" y1="22" x2="215" y2="22" stroke="#333" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#a5)"/>
        <text x="167" y="16" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">direction of travel</text>
        <rect x="80" y="40" width="26" height="14" fill="#8a8a8a" stroke="#333"/>
        <rect x="215" y="40" width="26" height="14" fill="#8a8a8a" stroke="#333"/>
        <text x="93" y="70" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">trailing</text>
        <text x="228" y="70" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">leading</text>
        <line x1="106" y1="47" x2="215" y2="47" stroke="#b43c3c" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,3"/>
        <text x="160" y="43" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b43c3c">220 km, measured to µm</text>
        <line x1="93" y1="76" x2="93" y2="133" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <line x1="228" y1="76" x2="228" y2="133" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
        <text x="170" y="196" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">the leading satellite reaches the mass first and is pulled forward</text>
      </svg>

  .container width:{50%}
    - Two identical satellites fly in the same orbit at about 490 km altitude, one about 220 km behind the other.
    - A microwave link measures the **distance between them** continuously, to within a few micrometres.
    - When the pair approaches a region of extra mass, the leading satellite is pulled forward first, and the distance increases. When the trailing satellite reaches the mass, the distance decreases again. A month of these distance changes is used to calculate a map of the global gravity field.
    - Mass changes that are not water are calculated with models and removed: the atmosphere, ocean tides, and the slow rise of the crust since the last ice age. Over Australia the last correction is small, which makes Australia a good place to use GRACE.
    - The result is given as **equivalent water thickness**, in millimetres of water, as the difference from the mean of a reference period.


# What the measurement can and cannot resolve

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    - Each monthly gravity field is a sum of spherical harmonics up to about degree 50. This resolves features of about **400 km** on the ground. Newer mass concentration (**mascon**) solutions come on finer grids, but the true resolution is still about the same.
    - So GRACE can resolve **large basins**. It cannot resolve individual farms or bores. Published studies use areas of at least $ 10^{5} $ to $ 10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $.
    - The Murray-Darling Basin, at $ 1.06\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{km^2} $, is close to the smallest area that GRACE can resolve with confidence. GRACE cannot resolve the Murrumbidgee catchment on its own.
    - Mass changes outside the basin appear in the basin estimate, and mass changes inside the basin appear outside it. This is called **leakage**, and correcting for it is most of the work in a GRACE study.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Three more limitations} background:{.lightred}
      - **No depth information.** Gravity gives the change in mass of a whole column. It cannot tell whether the water came from a reservoir, the soil or an aquifer 200 m down.
      - **One value per month at best.** Individual storms and recharge events are not visible.
      - **A gap in the record.** GRACE ran from 2002 to 2017 and GRACE-FO began in 2018. The eleven months between them are missing from every time series.

    In return, GRACE gives something that no network of bores can: a monthly total for the whole basin, over twenty years, that does not depend on where bores were drilled.


# From total storage to groundwater

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Gravity measures **all** the water in the column at once:

    $ \Delta \mathrm{TWS} = \Delta S_{snow} + \Delta S_{surface} + \Delta S_{soil} + \Delta S_{groundwater} $

    To get groundwater, subtract the other stores, each estimated from a separate data source:

    $ \Delta S_{gw} = \Delta \mathrm{TWS} - \Delta S_{surface} - \Delta S_{soil} $

    - Surface water comes from reservoir and river records. Soil moisture comes from a land surface model or from satellites. Snow is negligible in the Murray-Darling Basin.
    - Every error in these terms goes into the groundwater estimate. The groundwater estimate is therefore only as good as the soil moisture estimate.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{Does the subtraction work?} background:{.lightblue}
      Leblanc et al. (2009) tested it over the Murray-Darling Basin against ground measurements.

      - Annual GRACE storage against the sum of measured groundwater, surface water and soil moisture: **R = 0.94**.
      - GRACE minus soil moisture and surface water, against measured groundwater alone: **R = 0.92**.

      The groundwater estimate from GRACE agrees well with the bore measurements.

    **Assignments II and III ask you to do this calculation**, with the CSR mascons for total storage and the ESA CCI record for soil moisture.


# The Millennium Drought seen by GRACE

.row
  .container width:{50%}
    Murray-Darling Basin, 2001 to 2007, from Leblanc et al. (2009):

    | Store | Change | Over what period |
    |---|---|---|
    | Soil moisture | $ -80\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2003 |
    | Surface water | $ -12\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2003 |
    | Groundwater | $ -104\ \mathrm{km^3} $ | 2001 to 2007 |

    - Average total deficit over the GRACE record: **140 km³**. Peak deficit, January to March 2007: **240 km³**.
    - Soil moisture and surface water fell within **two years**, and then stopped falling because little was left. Groundwater was still falling after **six years**.
    - Rainfall returned to average in 2007. Groundwater storage did not recover.

  .container width:{50%}
    .box title:{What the sequence shows} background:{.lightblue}
      A drought moves through the water stores in order of their residence times. Rainfall falls first, then soil moisture within months, then streamflow, then groundwater over years. Groundwater also recovers last.

      By the rainfall record, the drought ended in 2007. By groundwater storage, it had not.

    - This is why we measure storage directly, instead of estimating it from rainfall.
    - Earlier parts of this lecture explain *why* groundwater responds slowly: low $ K $, long flow paths, and residence times of decades.


# What we learned

.row
  .container width:{100%}
    - **Porosity and conductivity are different properties.** Porosity tells you how much water the ground holds. Conductivity tells you how fast the water flows. Clay has one of the highest porosities and one of the lowest conductivities.
    - **Head sets the direction of flow.** $ h = z + P/\rho_w g $. In still water, head is constant but pressure is not. Head belongs to a point, so one site can have several heads.
    - **Darcy's law**, $ q = -K\,dh/dl $, was found by measurement, and the pore scale explains it: $ K \propto d^2 \rho_w g/\mu $. This result explains the range of $ K $ from gravel to clay, the grain-size formulas, and the split of $ K $ into permeability and fluid properties.
    - **Divide $ q $ by the effective porosity** to get the speed of the water. In fractured rock, the water can travel a hundred or more times faster than $ q $.
    - **Layering turns heterogeneity into anisotropy.** Along the bedding, use the arithmetic mean: the most conductive layer controls $ K_h $. Across the bedding, use the harmonic mean: the least conductive layer controls $ K_v $. Isotropic layers alone can give $ K_h/K_v = 10^6 $.
    - **Flow lines refract** at a contact. They cross a less conductive unit at right angles, and turn to run along a more conductive one.
    - **Field methods**: piezometer nests for vertical gradients, potentiometric maps for one aquifer at one time, hydrographs for change over time, and GRACE for the whole basin.

.fullspan
  .align{center}
    .container width:{60%} borderwidth:{1pt} padding:{0.2cm} background:{.lightred} radius:{0.5cm}
      Next week: **aquifers**. The different types of aquifer, and how much water they release when the head falls.
